.TH YACC 1 "2026-04-02" "IEEE/The Open Group" "POSIX Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
yacc \- yet another compiler compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yacc
[\fB\-dltv\fP] [\fB\-b\fP \fIfile_prefix\fP] [\fB\-p\fP \fIsym_prefix\fP] \fIgrammar\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B yacc
utility generates a C-language parser from a grammar specification.
It reads a grammar specification from the file specified by the
.I grammar
operand and generates an LALR(1) parser for that grammar.
The generated parser consists of a C-language function,
.BR yyparse (),
which parses the token stream produced by a lexical analyzer.
The parser is table-driven, utilizing state machines to resolve shift and reduce actions based on a one-token lookahead.
.PP
By default, the output parser is written to a file named
.IR y.tab.c .
The output file contains the
.BR yyparse ()
function, along with internal tables and action routines derived from the grammar.
If the grammar contains ambiguous rules,
.B yacc
reports conflicts (shift/reduce or reduce/reduce) to standard error and resolves them using default precedence rules unless explicitly defined in the grammar.
.SH OPTIONS
The
.B yacc
utility conforms to the POSIX.1-2017 standard and supports the following options:
.TP
.BI \-b " file_prefix"
Change the prefix of the generated output files to
.IR file_prefix
instead of the default
.BR y .
For example, if
.B \-b foo
is specified, the generated C parser file will be named
.IR foo.tab.c ,
and if the
.B \-d
option is also given, the header file will be named
.IR foo.tab.h .
Other files, such as the verbose output file, are similarly renamed (e.g.,
.IR foo.output ).
This option does not alter the names of the C symbols (such as
.BR yyparse )
generated by the utility.
.TP
.B \-d
Generate a C header file containing the `#define` statements that map token names in the grammar to numeric token identifiers.
This file is typically named
.I y.tab.h
(or modified by the
.B \-b
option).
It also includes the declaration of the
.B YYSTYPE
union type and the external declaration of the global variable
.IR yylval .
The generated header is typically included by the lexical analyzer (such as one generated by
.BR lex (1))
to synchronize token values.
.TP
.B \-l
Suppress the generation of `#line` directives in the output C file.
By default,
.B yacc
inserts `#line` directives into the generated code so that the C compiler or debugger maps errors back to the original
.I grammar
file.
Using this option ensures that all compiler errors and debugging information point strictly to lines within the generated
.I y.tab.c
file itself.
.TP
.BI \-p " sym_prefix"
Replace the default
.B yy
prefix applied to external parser symbols with the string specified by
.IR sym_prefix .
The symbols altered include:
.BR yyparse ,
.BR yylex ,
.BR yyerror ,
.BR yylval ,
.BR yychar ,
.BR yydebug ,
and
.BR yynerrs .
For example, using
.B \-p foo
will result in functions named
.BR fooparse ,
.BR foolex ,
and
.BR fooerror .
This is particularly useful when linking multiple parsers into a single program to prevent symbol name collisions.
The
.I sym_prefix
must be a valid C identifier.
This option does not affect the output file names.
.TP
.B \-t
Compile the generated parser with runtime debugging code enabled.
The generated C parser will include a set of trace statements controlled by the global integer variable
.IR yydebug .
To enable the diagnostic output at runtime,
.I yydebug
must be set to a non-zero value.
The debug output is written to standard error and details the parser's state transitions, token shifting, and rule reductions as they occur.
.TP
.B \-v
Generate a human-readable verbose description of the LALR(1) parsing tables and grammar state machine.
The description is written to a file named
.I y.output
(or
.IR file_prefix.output
if
.B \-b
is used).
The file contains all grammar rules, the set of items representing each parser state, the actions taken in each state on various lookahead tokens, and any shift/reduce or reduce/reduce conflicts that occurred.
.SH OPERANDS
The following operand is required:
.TP
.I grammar
The path to the file containing the grammar specification.
This file typically uses the
.B \.y
extension.
Only one grammar file may be specified per invocation.
If the application needs to read standard input, it must direct a file or pipe explicitly via the shell (standard input itself is not supported as an implicit operand).
.SH STDIN
Standard input is not used by POSIX conformant
.BR yacc .
The utility strictly expects a file argument.
.SH INPUT FILES
The input file must be a text file containing the grammar specification.
The file format is divided into three distinct sections, separated by the delimiter
.BR %% :
.PP
.nf
    declarations
    %%
    rules
    %%
    user_code
.fi
.PP
The third section is optional, and the second
.B %%
may be omitted if there is no user code.
Comments follow standard C syntax
.RB ( /*\  ...\  */ ).
.SS Declarations Section
The optional declarations section defines tokens, types, associativities, precedence rules, and raw C code inclusions.
.IP \(bu 3
C code to be included directly in the output parser must be enclosed within
.B %{
and
.BR %} .
This typically includes header files, global variables, and forward declarations.
.IP \(bu 3
.B %union \fI{ ... }\fP
declares the collection of data types used for the semantic values associated with tokens and nonterminals. It defines the structure of
.BR YYSTYPE .
.IP \(bu 3
.B %token
declares terminal symbols (tokens) returned by the lexical analyzer.
.IP \(bu 3
.B %type
defines the semantic value union member associated with a particular nonterminal.
.IP \(bu 3
.BR %left ,
.BR %right ,
and
.B %nonassoc
declare operators and enforce their associativity and precedence levels. Declarations appearing later in the section have higher precedence.
.IP \(bu 3
.B %start
specifies the grammar's root nonterminal. If omitted, the left-hand side of the first rule in the rules section is used.
.SS Rules Section
The required rules section contains the grammar productions written in Backus-Naur Form (BNF).
A rule has the form:
.PP
.nf
    nonterminal : production_1 { semantic_action_1 }
                | production_2 { semantic_action_2 }
                ;
.fi
.PP
The left-hand side nonterminal is separated from its productions by a colon
.RB ( : ),
and alternatives are separated by vertical bars
.RB ( | ).
A rule is terminated by a semicolon
.RB ( ; ).
.PP
Semantic actions are written in C enclosed in block braces
.RB ( { " ... " } ).
Within these actions,
.B $$
refers to the left-hand side semantic value, and
.B $1
through
.B $N
refer to the semantic values of the respective right-hand side symbols.
Type tags may be specified as
.BI $< tag >$
and
.BI $< tag >N
if a
.B %union
declaration was used.
.SS User Code Section
The optional user code section normally contains C functions necessary for the parser's operation, such as the entry point
.BR main (),
the lexical analyzer
.BR yylex (),
and the error reporting function
.BR yyerror ().
All text following the final
.B %%
is copied verbatim to the end of the generated C file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
The following environment variables affect the execution of
.BR yacc :
.TP
.B LANG
Provide a default value for the internationalization variables that are unset or null.
.TP
.B LC_ALL
If set to a non-empty string value, override the values of all the other internationalization variables.
.TP
.B LC_CTYPE
Determine the locale for the interpretation of sequences of bytes of text data as characters (for example, single-byte as opposed to multi-byte characters in arguments and input files).
.TP
.B LC_MESSAGES
Determine the locale that should be used to affect the format and contents of diagnostic messages written to standard error.
.TP
.B NLSPATH
Determine the location of message catalogs for the processing of
.BR LC_MESSAGES .
.SH ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
Default signal handling applies.
Upon receiving an interrupting signal (such as
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ),
.B yacc
attempts to clean up any temporary files before terminating with a non-zero exit status.
.SH STDOUT
Standard output is not used by the
.B yacc
utility.
.SH STDERR
Standard error is used exclusively for diagnostic messages, conflict warnings, error messages, and parser debugging output (if the generated parser was compiled from
.B \-t
and active).
Warnings regarding shift/reduce or reduce/reduce conflicts are written here in the format:
.PP
.nf
    conflicts: N shift/reduce, M reduce/reduce
.fi
.SH OUTPUT FILES
The following files may be generated by:
.TP
.I y.tab.c
The default C language parser file containing the
.BR yyparse ()
function and related tables. The name corresponds to
.IR file_prefix\fB.tab.c\fR
if
.B \-b
is used.
.TP
.I y.tab.h
The header file containing token `#define` macros and the
.B YYSTYPE
union declaration. Generated if
.B \-d
is specified. The name corresponds to
.IR file_prefix\fB.tab.h\fR
if
.B \-b
is used.
.TP
.I y.output
A verbose textual report describing the parsing automaton, item sets, and conflicts. Generated if
.B \-v
is specified. The name corresponds to
.IR file_prefix\fB.output\fR
if
.B \-b
is used.
.SH EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
The generated parser relies on three external functions commonly supplied by the user:
.TP
.B int yylex(void)
The lexical analyzer function that returns the next token's numeric identifier from the input stream.
It should set the global variable
.B yylval
to the semantic value of the token before returning.
A return value of zero or less indicates the end-of-file.
.TP
.B void yyerror(const char *msg)
The error handler function invoked by the parser upon encountering a syntax error.
It receives a descriptive string, usually
.BR "syntax error" .
.TP
.B int yyparse(void)
The main entry point in the generated file. It repeatedly calls
.BR yylex ()
and attempts to reduce productions. It returns
.B 0
upon successful parsing or
.B 1
if a syntax error is unrecoverable.
.PP
Global variables managed or accessed by the parser include:
.IP \(bu 3
.B yylval
- holds the semantic value of the current lookahead token.
.IP \(bu 3
.B yychar
- the integer identifier of the current lookahead token.
.IP \(bu 3
.B yynerrs
- a counter of syntax errors encountered during parsing.
.IP \(bu 3
.B yydebug
- an integer flag enabling runtime tracing if compiled with
.BR \-t .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The
.B yacc
utility exits with one of the following values:
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
The parser was generated properly. Note that grammar conflicts result in warnings but do not cause a non-zero exit status.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred, such as invalid command-line options, inability to read the grammar file, a fatal syntax error in the grammar itself, or an I/O error writing output files.
.SH CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
If any error occurs during the processing of the grammar file (such as a syntax error within the grammar definition), parsing stops immediately, diagnostic messages are written to standard error, and the exit status is set to a value greater than zero. No output files are produced or updated.
Shift/reduce and reduce/reduce conflicts in the grammar produce warnings on standard error, but the parser is still generated, and the exit status remains zero unless other fatal errors exist.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
To write a successful parser, the developer should keep the grammar clear and structured, avoid deep ambiguity, and leverage precedence declarations
.RB ( %left ,
.BR %right )
judiciously to resolve classic shift/reduce conflicts such as the "dangling-else" problem.
It is highly recommended to integrate the
.B yacc
parser generator with a companion lexical generator like
.BR lex (1)
or
.BR flex (1).
.PP
When designing an error recovery strategy, place the special token
.B error
in grammatical rules where synchronization can be achieved. During a syntax error, the parser shifts the
.B error
token and attempts to resume normal parsing based on upcoming lookahead tokens, potentially discarding stack frames or tokens until parity is reached.
Macros such as
.B yyerrok
(reset the error state explicitly) and
.B yyclearin
(discard the current lookahead) can be used within error reduction actions.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Calculator Example
.nf
%{
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int yylex(void);
void yyerror(const char *s);
%}

%token NUMBER
%left '+' '\-'
%left '*' '/'

%%
calc:
    /* empty */
    | calc expr '\\n' { printf("= %d\\n", $2); }
    ;

expr:
    NUMBER          { $$ = $1; }
    | expr '+' expr { $$ = $1 + $3; }
    | expr '\-' expr { $$ = $1 \- $3; }
    | expr '*' expr { $$ = $1 * $3; }
    | expr '/' expr { $$ = $1 / $3; }
    ;
%%

void yyerror(const char *s) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\\n", s);
}

int main(void) {
    return yyparse();
}
.fi
.SS Command Invocation
Generate a parser along with its header file:
.HP
.B yacc \-d calc.y
.PP
Generate a parser with a custom prefix and verbose reporting:
.HP
.B yacc \-v \-d \-b mycalc \-p mc calc.y
.SH RATIONALE
The
.B yacc
utility implements the standard LALR(1) parser generation algorithm.
LALR(1) stands for "Look-Ahead LR parser with a 1-token lookahead."
This paradigm strikes a pragmatic balance between the vast expressive power of full LR(k) grammars and the manageable state-machine sizes produced by SLR generation, resulting in parsers that are adequate for compiling nearly all practical programming languages while maintaining high parsing speed.
.PP
The default output conventions (i.e.,
.IR y.tab.c ,
.IR y.tab.h )
reflect
.BR yacc 's
origins in the 1970s by Stephen C. Johnson at Bell Labs. Replacing or maintaining these filenames ensures deep compatibility with legacy Makefiles across POSIX-compliant systems.
The decision to enforce a file argument rather than reading standard input adheres to modern POSIX expectations, preventing ambiguous piping scenarios and ensuring consistent behavior directly interacting with the local filesystem.
